\subsection{Threshold Recommendation}

For the main diagnostic (Task~B2), we recommend $\nve \geq 1.5\times$ as the operating threshold for three reasons:

\begin{enumerate}[nosep,leftmargin=*]
\item \textbf{Perfect classification.} The $1.5\times$ threshold achieves 100\% accuracy on the $n{=}11$ factorial set: all four CIVO cases (006, 059, 008d, 009b) are detected, and no non-CIVO experiment exceeds the threshold (AUROC${=}1.000$, F1${=}100\%$).
\item \textbf{Maximum recall with margin.} The lowest CIVO-positive NVE ($1.62\times$, 009b) clears the threshold by $0.12\times$; the highest negative ($1.00\times$, 001) sits $0.50\times$ below it.
\item \textbf{Robustness.} The $1.5\times$ threshold achieves perfect classification across the extended $n{=}17$ two-environment evaluation (Table~\ref{tab:sensitivity_b2_full}), with the nearest positive--negative gap widening further ($1.53\times$ vs.\ $1.04\times$).
\end{enumerate}

For Task~B1 (sustained CIVO only), the optimal threshold $\nve \geq 1.7\times$ achieves $10/11$ accuracy ($90.9\%$, 95\% CI: [58.7\%, 99.8\%]); the sole false positive is exp\_059 (NVE${=}4.72\times$, self-correcting CIVO classified as B1-negative).

