\end{figure}

We label a configuration as CIVO-positive if and only if (i)~it uses a trained $\dt$-embedding with multi-rate data exposure, and (ii)~NVE exceeds $1.5\times$ with co-occurring entropy collapse. Configurations exhibiting high NVE through other mechanisms (gradient truncation side effects, value anchor pollution) are labeled non-CIVO.

The two false positives are mechanistically distinct from CIVO: (1)~RAVE anchor pollution (exp\_028, the EMA anchor becomes inflated, producing overestimation through a different pathway); (2)~obs-decoder SGS-induced value distortion (exp\_025 seed~2, blocking observation decoder gradients damages the RSSM latent space). Both produce NVE $> 1.5\times$ through non-CIVO mechanisms, indicating that the NVE diagnostic detects value overestimation broadly rather than CIVO specifically---a feature rather than a limitation for practitioners monitoring deployment health. The closest sub-threshold case (008f seed~4, NVE${=}1.41\times$) lacks $\dt$-conditioning entirely ($d{=}0$); its elevated NVE reflects transient value noise that self-corrects without the positive feedback loop characteristic of CIVO. The closest above-threshold positive (008d seed~3, NVE${=}1.53\times$) exhibits the full CIVO causal chain: trained $\dt$-embedding, entropy decrease, and evaluation collapse to 14\% of baseline.

\paragraph{Comprehensive validation ($n{=}56$).}
Expanding the evaluation set to all classifiable experiments yields $n{=}56$ ($n_{\text{pos}}{=}24$, $n_{\text{neg}}{=}32$), spanning Walker Walk, Cheetah Run, and multiple intervention configurations (SGS, TCN, dual-critic, frozen embedding). At the $1.5\times$ threshold: AUROC${=}0.990$, precision${=}0.923$, recall${=}1.000$, F1${=}0.960$, accuracy${=}54/56$. The two false positives are: exp\_047 ($1.71\times$, frozen embedding transient) and exp\_057 ($1.88\times$, frozen embedding transient). All CIVO-positive configurations are correctly detected (FN${=}0$), with CIVO-positive NVE ranging from $1.53\times$ (008d seed~3, late-onset) to $70.64\times$ (010b seed~0, Cheetah). The substantial increase in sample size from $n{=}32$ to $n{=}56$ strengthens confidence in the $1.5\times$ threshold as a robust diagnostic boundary.

\subsection{NVE Normalization Robustness}
