\subsection{Leave-One-Config-Out Cross-Validation}
\label{sec:nve_loco}

\paragraph{Leave-One-Config-Out Cross-Validation.}
To rule out threshold overfitting to any particular experimental configuration, we perform leave-one-config-out (LOCO) cross-validation over $K{=}12$ configuration groups (e.g., \texttt{008d\_civo\_trigger}, \texttt{frozen\_dt\_emb}, \texttt{tcn}; $n{=}56$ total). Each fold holds out all experiments from one configuration, selects the F1-maximizing threshold on the remaining $K{-}1$ groups, and evaluates on the held-out group.

In $10/12$ folds, the training-optimal threshold falls within $\pm 0.05$ of $\theta{=}1.5\times$ (mean $1.61 \pm 0.23$, range $[1.47, 2.12]$). The two folds selecting a higher threshold ($\theta{=}2.1$; \texttt{008d\_civo\_trigger} and \texttt{tcn}, both predominantly CIVO-positive) perform \emph{worse} on their held-out sets than $\theta{=}1.5$ (accuracy $0.78$/$0.88$ vs.\ $1.00$/$1.00$), confirming that the fixed threshold generalizes better than per-fold optimization in these cases. Cross-validated accuracy at $\theta{=}1.5$ is $54/56 = 96.4\%$, identical to the in-sample result, with train AUROC consistently high across folds ($0.988 \pm 0.003$). The two misclassifications are the same mechanistically distinct false positives identified in \S\ref{sec:roc_analysis}: two frozen-embedding transients (exp\_047, exp\_057). No CIVO-positive configuration is missed in any fold (FN${=}0$ across all 12 folds).

