\subsection{Threshold Recommendation}

For the main diagnostic (Task~B2), we recommend $\nve \geq 1.5\times$ as the operating threshold for three reasons:

\begin{enumerate}[nosep,leftmargin=*]
\item \textbf{Perfect classification.} The $1.5\times$ threshold achieves 100\% accuracy on the $n{=}11$ factorial set: all four CIVO cases (006, 059, 008d, 009b) are detected, and no non-CIVO experiment exceeds the threshold (AUROC${=}1.000$, F1${=}100\%$).
\item \textbf{Maximum recall with margin.} The lowest CIVO-positive NVE ($1.62\times$, 009b) clears the threshold by $0.12\times$; the highest negative ($1.00\times$, 001) sits $0.50\times$ below it.
\item \textbf{Robustness.} The $1.5\times$ threshold achieves perfect classification across the extended $n{=}17$ two-environment evaluation (Table~\ref{tab:sensitivity_b2_full}), with the nearest positive--negative gap widening further ($1.53\times$ vs.\ $1.04\times$).
\end{enumerate}

For Task~B1 (sustained CIVO only), the optimal threshold $\nve \geq 1.7\times$ achieves $10/11$ accuracy ($90.9\%$, 95\% CI: [58.7\%, 99.8\%]); the sole false positive is exp\_059 (NVE${=}4.72\times$, self-correcting CIVO classified as B1-negative).

\subsection{Expanded ROC Analysis}
\label{sec:roc_analysis}

The original $n{=}17$ analysis (Tables~\ref{tab:sensitivity_b1}--\ref{tab:sensitivity_b2_full}) predates the SGS, RAVE, and TCN experiments. We extend the NVE diagnostic evaluation to $n{=}56$ configurations spanning all tested interventions. Figure~\ref{fig:nve_roc} shows the receiver operating characteristic curve with 95\% bootstrap confidence interval (10{,}000 resamples).

\begin{figure}[h]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.7\columnwidth]{../../artifacts/figures/nve_roc_curve.pdf}
\caption{NVE ROC curve ($n{=}56$ configurations, AUROC${=}0.990$, comprehensive validation). The preset $\nve \geq 1.5\times$ operating point (star) achieves TPR${=}1.00$, FPR${=}0.06$, accuracy${=}54/56$, F1${=}0.960$. Two false positives arise from frozen embedding transients (047, $1.71\times$; 057, $1.88\times$). Threshold calibrated and validated post-hoc; cross-architecture validation deferred.}
\label{fig:nve_roc}
\end{figure}

\begin{figure}[h]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.7\columnwidth]{../../artifacts/figures/nve_threshold_prf.pdf}
\caption{Precision, Recall, and F1 as a function of NVE threshold ($n{=}56$ configurations). Peak F1${=}0.960$ at $\tau{=}1.5\times$, where recall remains perfect ($1.00$) and precision${=}0.923$. The plateau of high F1 across $\tau \in [1.5, 1.7]$ indicates robustness to threshold selection.}
\label{fig:nve_prf}
\end{figure}

We label a configuration as CIVO-positive if and only if (i)~it uses a trained $\dt$-embedding with multi-rate data exposure, and (ii)~NVE exceeds $1.5\times$ with co-occurring entropy collapse. Configurations exhibiting high NVE through other mechanisms (gradient truncation side effects, value anchor pollution) are labeled non-CIVO.

The two false positives are mechanistically distinct from CIVO: (1)~RAVE anchor pollution (exp\_028, the EMA anchor becomes inflated, producing overestimation through a different pathway); (2)~obs-decoder SGS-induced value distortion (exp\_025 seed~2, blocking observation decoder gradients damages the RSSM latent space). Both produce NVE $> 1.5\times$ through non-CIVO mechanisms, indicating that the NVE diagnostic detects value overestimation broadly rather than CIVO specifically---a feature rather than a limitation for practitioners monitoring deployment health. The closest sub-threshold case (008f seed~4, NVE${=}1.41\times$) lacks $\dt$-conditioning entirely ($d{=}0$); its elevated NVE reflects transient value noise that self-corrects without the positive feedback loop characteristic of CIVO. The closest above-threshold positive (008d seed~3, NVE${=}1.53\times$) exhibits the full CIVO causal chain: trained $\dt$-embedding, entropy decrease, and evaluation collapse to 14\% of baseline.

\paragraph{Comprehensive validation ($n{=}56$).}
Expanding the evaluation set to all classifiable experiments yields $n{=}56$ ($n_{\text{pos}}{=}24$, $n_{\text{neg}}{=}32$), spanning Walker Walk, Cheetah Run, and multiple intervention configurations (SGS, TCN, dual-critic, frozen embedding). At the $1.5\times$ threshold: AUROC${=}0.990$, precision${=}0.923$, recall${=}1.000$, F1${=}0.960$, accuracy${=}54/56$. The two false positives are: exp\_047 ($1.71\times$, frozen embedding transient) and exp\_057 ($1.88\times$, frozen embedding transient). All CIVO-positive configurations are correctly detected (FN${=}0$), with CIVO-positive NVE ranging from $1.53\times$ (008d seed~3, late-onset) to $70.64\times$ (010b seed~0, Cheetah). The substantial increase in sample size from $n{=}32$ to $n{=}56$ strengthens confidence in the $1.5\times$ threshold as a robust diagnostic boundary.

